To convert Markdown-formatted text to HTML in C++, you can create a simple function that handles basic Markdown syntax like headers, bold, italic, and links. Below is an example of such a function:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;

    // Convert headers
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^# (.*)"), "<h1>$1</h1>");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^## (.*)"), "<h2>$1</h2>");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^### (.*)"), "<h3>$1</h3>");

    // Convert bold
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*"), "<strong>$1</strong>");

    // Convert italic
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\*(.*?)\\*"), "<em>$1</em>");

    // Convert links
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\[(.*?)\\]\\((.*?)\\)"), "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to find patterns in the Markdown text and replace them with corresponding HTML tags. Note that this is a very basic implementation and does not cover all Markdown features or edge cases. For more complex conversions, consider using a dedicated library like `marked` (though it's primarily for JavaScript).

